            "review_title": "Review at Tre Place",
            "review_text": "johngsexton\nNorth Palm Beach, Florida20 contributions\n0\nLunch at Tre Place\nSep 2015 • Couples\nBeautiful location on Red River, quite secluded, and bit desolate for lunch, few patrons scattered over several large seating areas inside and out. This place is huge venue and tranquil gardens, can seat several hundred. You can watch barges go by into and out of Hanoi. Cheap by American standards, and not much more than loud popular Bia Hoi's with similar fare but much more elegant and finely presented and much larger selection. White cloth tablecloths in beautiful open building we sit with black cloth runners embroidered with silk lotus flowers. (See photo). Large grilled tiger shrimp (Tom Su) fresh and excellent. Brought to table and then \"skinned\" table side, (see photos, before and after shelling). Grilled squid was very good as it is quite popular and excellent all over Vietnam. Vegetables as usual are unique to Vietnam and most are excellent, most stir fried with garlic, but not here where some are served just boiled and bit bland for my taste. I have spoken about some of the specific vegetables before in earlier posts of Vietnamese restaurants with no English translation. Many when you can google them show up as medicinal herbs! Regardless, try the greens in variety, I find them mostly delightful. Tiny clam soup (cảnh ngạo chua) was especially good here today, I've had clam soups elsewhere in Hanoi, but this version without shells in soup and much smaller has to be tried especially with fresh chilli peppers added for a spicy treat. Soup is clear broth with fresh tomato, pineapple, bean sprouts, parsley before the added fresh chilis. \"Trung duct thit\" is like a western style frittata cooked mostly of folded fried eggs with tiny fragments of scallion and minced pork, interesting for lunch for me, but Vietnamese fiancé says good for dinner too served with rice. This Classic Vietnamese cuisine can not be much better anywhere, especially for westerners expecting fresh wholesome food, clean dishes and chopsticks unlike so many other expensive restaurants in Hanoi of this genre where you wonder about bacteria content on utensils, bowls and plates. Classic Vietnamese Food here is indeed as good or better than most any attempt at Western cuisine found in Hanoi, price is very reasonable, atmosphere could not be better. When in Hanoi, eat Hanoi style food and this is great place to compare, I'm sure if you try enough, you will have a great experience!\nRead more\nWritten September 15, 2015",
            "rating": 5.0,
            "review_date": "2026-10-15",
            "travel_date": "Recent",
            "feature_depth_status": "Schema-Full-Features-Validated"
